## std::scalar — scalar primitive method-home class (Int).
##
## Zero-Rust-stdlib migration, Phase 3 (Option C). `class Int` below is
## the METHOD-HOME class for the `Ty::Int` primitive head: it carries
## the FFI alias decls (`Int_to_s` → `ruxen_int_to_string`, etc.) AND
## the true surface return types delegated via the builtin-receiver
## bridge (`bridge_builtin_method`, reached because
## `builtin_bridge::is_delegated_head` includes `Ty::Int`).
##
## CRITICAL INVARIANT — this class changes only the METHOD-HOME, NOT the
## value representation. `Int` is registered as `TypeAlias { target:
## Ty::Int }` by `register_primitives`; the bootstrap merge anchors this
## class onto the same DefId and stomps it to `Class`, but
## `resolve_type_expr`'s `primitive_class_ty` fast-path returns the
## primitive head for a bare `let x: Int` annotation, so literals,
## arithmetic, comparisons, and the C ABI / runtime repr are unchanged.
## The receiver is passed by value (i64) at the call site — `42.to_s`.
##
## ## ABI notes — why ONLY Int (not Float/Bool/Char)
##
## The instance-method receiver is prepended to the derived FFI param
## list as `Ty::Class { name: "Int" }` (`register_class_lib_method_in`),
## which `ty_to_cranelift` lowers to a pointer-sized **I64**. That
## happens to MATCH `ruxen_int_to_string(int64_t)` / `ruxen_int_to_f(
## int64_t)` exactly, so Int migrates cleanly (the parity guard
## `tests/runtime_abi_derivation.rs` confirms it).
##
## Float / Bool / Char do NOT migrate — the prepended class receiver is
## always I64, but their C symbols take a `double` (Float → F64) or an
## `int64_t` that a `class Bool` / `class Char` receiver would derive to
## I8 / I32. The derived receiver width would contradict the C ABI and
## fail the parity guard. They stay Rust residuals in
## `typeck/method_resolvers/numeric.rs` — same divergence rule as
## `String.remove`. FOLLOW-UP: teach the receiver-prepend to use the
## primitive head (or widen the C receivers) and migrate Float/Bool/Char.
## `USize.to_s` / `to_string` likewise stay residual — there is no
## `class USize` (it shares `ruxen_int_to_string` with Int).
class Int
  lib "../../string/runtime/string.c"
    def to_string as "ruxen_int_to_string" -> String
    def to_s as "ruxen_int_to_string" -> String
    def to_f as "ruxen_int_to_f" -> Float
  end
end

## Feature A — scalar method-homes for Float / Bool / Char / USize.
##
## These migrated once `primitive_ffi_receiver_ty` taught the receiver-
## prepend to use the C symbol's TRUE register class:
##   * `Float` → F64 (its `ruxen_float_*` symbols take a `double`);
##   * `Bool` / `Char` / `USize` → I64 (their `ruxen_*` symbols take an
##     `int64_t`, so the default pointer-sized class receiver already
##     matches — the migration is the `.rx` shell + `primitive_class_ty`
##     + `is_delegated_head` entries, no width change for these three).
## The parity guard `tests/runtime_abi_derivation.rs` pins every derived
## width against the frozen `runtime_signature` table.
class Float
  lib "../../string/runtime/string.c"
    def to_string as "ruxen_float_to_string" -> String
    def to_s as "ruxen_float_to_string" -> String
    def to_i as "ruxen_float_to_i" -> Int
  end
end

class Bool
  lib "../../string/runtime/string.c"
    def to_string as "ruxen_bool_to_string" -> String
    def to_s as "ruxen_bool_to_string" -> String
  end
end

class Char
  lib "../../string/runtime/string.c"
    def to_s as "ruxen_char_to_string" -> String
    def to_string as "ruxen_char_to_string" -> String
  end
end

class USize
  lib "../../string/runtime/string.c"
    def to_string as "ruxen_int_to_string" -> String
    def to_s as "ruxen_int_to_string" -> String
  end
end
